hi all, welcome to this episode of software engineering radio.

11
00:01:01,670 --> 00:01:09,190
our guest today is dermot mcdonald, he is a lecturer in social sciences at the university of west scotland.

12
00:01:09,190 --> 00:01:16,149
dermot graduated with a phd from the faculty of social sciences at the university of sterling in scotland.

13
00:01:16,149 --> 00:01:23,830
his research employs large-scale administrative datasets, this has led dermot on the path of web scraping.

14
00:01:23,830 --> 00:01:28,870
he has run webinars and published these on youtube to share his experiences and educate the community.

15
00:01:28,870 --> 00:01:34,550
on what a developer or data scientist must evaluate before starting out on a web scraping project.

16
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:40,950
as well as what they should learn and watch out for, and finally the challenges that they may encounter.

17
00:01:40,960 --> 00:01:44,710
then with so great to have you on the show.

18
00:01:44,710 --> 00:01:47,429
is there anything else you'd like to add to your bio before we get started?

19
00:01:47,439 --> 00:01:50,469
nope, that's an excellent introduction, thank you so much.

20
00:01:50,479 --> 00:01:55,270
great, so big picture, let's spend a little bit of time on that.

21
00:01:55,280 --> 00:02:03,830
and my first question would be what's the difference between screen scraping, web scraping and crawling?

22
00:02:03,840 --> 00:02:07,590
well, i think there are three varieties of the same approach.

23
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:18,470
web scraping is traditionally where we try and collect information, particularly text and often tables maybe images, from a website using some computational means.

24
00:02:18,480 --> 00:02:23,670
screen scraping is roughly the same, but i guess a bit more of a broader term.

25
00:02:23,670 --> 00:02:27,350
for collecting all of the information that you see on a screen from a website.

26
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:35,670
crawling is is very similar, but in that instance we're less interested in the content that's on the web page or the website.

27
00:02:35,670 --> 00:02:42,550
i'm more interested in the links that exist on a website, so crawling is about finding out how websites are connected together.

28
00:02:42,560 --> 00:02:46,309
how would crawling and web scraping be related?

29
00:02:46,319 --> 00:02:52,550
you definitely need to find the sites you need to scrape first, absolutely.

30
00:02:52,550 --> 00:03:00,229
they've got different purposes, but they have a common first step which is requesting the url of a web page in the first instance.

31
00:03:00,239 --> 00:03:06,470
with web scraping the next step is to collect the text or the the video or image information on the web page.

32
00:03:06,470 --> 00:03:14,630
with crawling what you're interested in are all of the hyperlinks that exist on that web page and where they link to going forward.

33
00:03:14,640 --> 00:03:24,869
so we'll get into some of the use cases, but before that why use web scraping in this day and age?

34
00:03:24,869 --> 00:03:28,070
with the prevalent apis provided by most vendors.

35
00:03:28,080 --> 00:03:36,710
that's a good question, apis are very important development in general for the public and for developers.

36
00:03:36,710 --> 00:03:42,789
as academics they're useful, but they don't provide the full spectrum of information that we may be interested in for research purposes.

37
00:03:42,799 --> 00:03:46,789
so many public services for example are accessed through websites.

38
00:03:46,799 --> 00:03:51,750
they provide lots of interesting information on policies and on statistics for example.

39
00:03:51,750 --> 00:03:54,070
and these web pages change quite frequently.

40
00:03:54,080 --> 00:04:01,589
through an api you can get maybe some of the same information, but of course it's restricted to whatever the data provider thinks you need.

41
00:04:01,599 --> 00:04:11,670
so in essence it's about what you think you may need in total to do your research for example, versus what's available from the data provider based on their policies.

42
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:15,750
okay, now let's drill in some other use cases.

43
00:04:15,750 --> 00:04:20,550
what in your mind are the key use cases for which web scraping isn't employed, and what was yours?

44
00:04:20,560 --> 00:04:24,230
well i begin with mine as an academic and as a researcher.

45
00:04:24,230 --> 00:04:28,469
i'm interested in large-scale administrative data about non-profits around the world.

46
00:04:28,479 --> 00:04:37,030
there's lots of different regulators of these organizations, and many do provide data downloads in common open source formats.

47
00:04:37,040 --> 00:04:44,710
however there's lots of information about these sectors that the regulator holds but doesn't necessarily make available in their data download.

48
00:04:44,710 --> 00:04:52,790
so for example the people running these organizations, that information is typically available on the regulator's website but not in the data download.

49
00:04:52,800 --> 00:04:57,990
so a good use case for me as a researcher, if i want to analyze how these organizations are governed.

50
00:04:57,990 --> 00:05:01,270
i need to know who sits on the board of these organizations.

51
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:09,670
so for me often the use case in academia and in research is that the value-added richer information we need for our research exists on web pages.

52
00:05:09,670 --> 00:05:13,830
but not necessarily in the publicly available data downloads.

53
00:05:13,830 --> 00:05:19,189
and i think this is a common use case across industry and potentially for personal use also.

54
00:05:19,189 --> 00:05:28,550
that the value added and richest information is available on websites but has not necessarily been packaged nicely as a data download.

55
00:05:28,560 --> 00:05:33,510
can you start with an actual problem that you solve?

56
00:05:33,510 --> 00:05:37,909
you hinted at one, but if you can guide us through the entire issue.

57
00:05:37,909 --> 00:05:41,670
what was you know did something unexpected happen as you were trying to scrape the information?

58
00:05:41,670 --> 00:05:44,390
what was the purpose just to get us started?

59
00:05:44,400 --> 00:05:50,070
absolutely, one particular jurisdiction i'm interested in is australia.

60
00:05:50,070 --> 00:05:55,029
it has quite a vibrant non-profit sector known as charities in that jurisdiction.

61
00:05:55,039 --> 00:05:58,629
and i was interested in the people who governed these organizations.

62
00:05:58,639 --> 00:06:04,790
now there is some limited information on these people in the publicly available data download.

63
00:06:04,790 --> 00:06:13,510
but the value-added information on the web page shows how these trustees are also on the board of other nonprofits and on the board of other organizations.

64
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:17,270
so those network connections i was particularly interested in australia.

65
00:06:17,270 --> 00:06:25,430
so that led me to develop a reasonably simple web scraping application that will get me the trusty information for australian non-profits.

66
00:06:25,440 --> 00:06:30,230
there's some common approaches and techniques i'm sure we'll get into.

67
00:06:30,230 --> 00:06:36,390
but one particular challenge was the regulator's website does have an idea of who's making requests for their web pages.

68
00:06:36,400 --> 00:06:43,510
and i haven't counted exactly, but every one or two thousand requests it would block that ip address.

69
00:06:43,510 --> 00:06:47,990
so i was setting my scraper up at night, which would be the morning over there for me.

70
00:06:47,990 --> 00:06:54,550
i was assuming it was running and i would come back in the morning and would find that my script had stopped working midway through the night.

71
00:06:54,560 --> 00:06:58,469
so that led me to build in some protections and some conditionals.

72
00:06:58,469 --> 00:07:06,710
that meant that every couple of hundred requests i would send my web scraping application to sleep for five ten minutes and then start again.

73
00:07:06,720 --> 00:07:10,150
so was this the first time you had done web scraping?

74
00:07:10,160 --> 00:07:13,270
no, i'd say this is probably somewhere in in the middle.

75
00:07:13,280 --> 00:07:16,870
my first experience of this was quite simple.

76
00:07:16,870 --> 00:07:19,589
i was on strike from my university and fighting for our pensions.

77
00:07:19,599 --> 00:07:24,550
i had two weeks and a colleague had been using python for a different application.

78
00:07:24,560 --> 00:07:30,550
and i thought i would try and access some data that looked particularly interesting back in my home country of the republic of ireland.

79
00:07:30,560 --> 00:07:39,589
so i said i sat there for two weeks tried to learn some python quite slowly and tried to download some data from an api.

80
00:07:39,589 --> 00:07:44,309
but what i quickly realized in my field of non-profit studies is that there aren't too many apis.

81
00:07:44,309 --> 00:07:48,309
but there are lots of websites with lots of rich information on these organizations.

82
00:07:48,309 --> 00:07:53,350
and that led me to employ web scraping quite frequently in my research.

83
00:07:53,360 --> 00:08:01,430
so there must be a reason though why these websites don't actually provide all this data as part of their apis.

84
00:08:01,440 --> 00:08:03,909
so is it actually legal to scrape?

85
00:08:03,909 --> 00:08:07,350
so what is legal and what's not legal to screen?

86
00:08:07,360 --> 00:08:12,710
it would be lovely if there was a very clear distinction between which websites were legal and which were not.

87
00:08:12,720 --> 00:08:18,629
in the uk for example there isn't a specific piece of legislation that forbids web scraping.

88
00:08:18,639 --> 00:08:27,189
but what it comes under are copyright legislation, intellectual property legislation, and data protection legislation.

89
00:08:27,189 --> 00:08:31,990
now that's not the case in every jurisdiction, it varies, but those are the common issues you come across.

90
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:38,230
it's less to do with the fact that you can't in an automated manner collect information from websites.

91
00:08:38,230 --> 00:08:45,910
though sometimes some websites terms and conditions say you cannot have a computational means of collecting data from the website.

92
00:08:45,920 --> 00:08:51,110
but in in general it's not about not being able to computationally collect the data.

93
00:08:51,110 --> 00:08:57,910
it's there's restrictions on what you can do with the data having collected it through your web scraper.

94
00:08:57,910 --> 00:09:02,470
so that's the real barrier, particularly for me in the uk and particularly the applications i have in mind.

95
00:09:02,480 --> 00:09:06,230
it's the restrictions on what i can do with the data.

96
00:09:06,230 --> 00:09:13,110
so i may be able to technically and legally scrape it, but i might be able to do any analysis or repackage it or share it in some findings.

97
00:09:13,120 --> 00:09:16,470
so do you first check the terms and conditions?

98
00:09:16,470 --> 00:09:21,110
does your scraper first you know parse through the terms and conditions to the site?

99
00:09:21,120 --> 00:09:25,110
this is actually one of the manual tasks associated with web scraping.

100
00:09:25,110 --> 00:09:29,990
in fact it's the detective work that you have to do to get your web scrapers set up.

101
00:09:29,990 --> 00:09:37,030
so it's not actually a technical task or a computational task, it's simply clicking on the website's terms of service or terms of conditions.

102
00:09:37,040 --> 00:09:47,350
usually a link found near the bottom of web pages, and you have to read them and say does this website specifically forbid automated scraping of of their web pages?

103
00:09:47,360 --> 00:09:53,509
if it does then you may usually write to that website and ask for their permission to run a scraper.

104
00:09:53,509 --> 00:09:58,230
sometimes they do say yes, you often it's a blanket statement that you're not allowed webscrape.

105
00:09:58,230 --> 00:10:06,069
but if you have a good public interest reason as an academic for example you may get permission.

106
00:10:06,069 --> 00:10:14,790
but often websites aren't explicit in banning web scraping, but they will have lots of conditions about the use of the data you find on the web pages.

107
00:10:14,790 --> 00:10:19,750
that's usually the biggest obstacle to overcome in terms of the terms and conditions.

108
00:10:19,750 --> 00:10:27,350
are they different if it's a public page versus a page that's protected by user auth, like you actually logged in?

109
00:10:27,360 --> 00:10:33,110
yes, there is a distinction between those different levels of access to pages.

110
00:10:33,110 --> 00:10:38,389
so in general web script you may be just forbidden by the terms of service in general.

111
00:10:38,399 --> 00:10:48,870
often if information is accessible via web scraping then that usually does not apply to information held behind authentication.

112
00:10:48,870 --> 00:10:56,550
so private pages, members only areas, they're usually restricted from your web scraping activities, and often for good reason.

113
00:10:56,550 --> 00:10:58,630
and it's not something i've ever tried to overcome.

114
00:10:58,630 --> 00:11:02,069
so there are technical means of doing so. that makes sense.

115
00:11:02,079 --> 00:11:08,310
let's now talk about the technology that you used to employ web scraping.

116
00:11:08,320 --> 00:11:13,509
so let's start with the challenges, the challenges of course.

117
00:11:13,519 --> 00:11:20,710
when i began learning to conduct web scraping it began as an intellectual pursuit.

118
00:11:20,710 --> 00:11:25,829
and in social sciences there's increasing use of computational approaches in our data collection and data analysis methods.

119
00:11:25,839 --> 00:11:29,829
one way of doing that is to write your own programming applications.

120
00:11:29,829 --> 00:11:37,750
so instead of using a software out of a box so to speak, i'll write a web scraper from scratch using the python programming language.

121
00:11:37,760 --> 00:11:43,990
of course the natural first challenge is you're not trained as a developer or as a programmer.

122
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:48,310
and you don't have those ingrained good practices in terms of writing code.

123
00:11:48,310 --> 00:11:55,030
so for us as social scientists in particular we call it the grilled cheese methodology.

124
00:11:55,030 --> 00:11:57,110
which is that your programs just have to be good enough.

125
00:11:57,120 --> 00:12:03,350
and you're not too focused on performance and shaving micro seconds off the performance of your web scraper.

126
00:12:03,360 --> 00:12:08,949
your focus on making sure it collects the data you want and does so when you need to.

127
00:12:08,949 --> 00:12:13,030
so the first challenge is to write effective code, if it's not necessarily efficient.

128
00:12:13,040 --> 00:12:17,990
but i guess if you are a developer you will be focused on efficiency.

129
00:12:17,990 --> 00:12:23,350
also the second major challenge is the detective work i outlined earlier.

130
00:12:23,360 --> 00:12:29,829
often in terms of conditions or terms of service of a web page are not entirely clear.

131
00:12:29,839 --> 00:12:36,470
they may not expressly prohibit web scraping, but they may have lots of clauses around you know.

132
00:12:36,470 --> 00:12:40,150
you may not download or use this data for your own purposes and so on.

133
00:12:40,160 --> 00:12:44,870
so you may be technically able to collect the data, but you may be in a bit of a bind.

134
00:12:44,870 --> 00:12:48,710
in terms of what you can actually do with the data once you've downloaded it.

135
00:12:48,720 --> 00:12:54,230
the third challenge is building in some reliability into your data collection activities.

136
00:12:54,230 --> 00:13:04,069
this is particularly important in my area as i'm interested in public bodies and regulators whose web pages tend to update very very quickly.

137
00:13:04,069 --> 00:13:07,110
often on a daily basis as new information comes in.

138
00:13:07,110 --> 00:13:12,870
so i need to ensure not just that i know how to write a web scraper and to direct it to collect useful information.

139
00:13:12,880 --> 00:13:17,190
it then brings me into more software applications and system software.

140
00:13:17,190 --> 00:13:23,670
where i need to either have a personal server that's running and then i need to maintain that as well to collect data.

141
00:13:23,670 --> 00:13:29,590
and it brings me into a couple of other areas that are not natural and i think to a non-developer and a non-programmer.

142
00:13:29,600 --> 00:13:37,750
so i'd see those as the the three main obstacles and challenges particularly for a non-programmer to overcome when web scraping.

143
00:13:37,760 --> 00:13:41,750
yeah these are certainly challenges even for somebody that's experienced.

144
00:13:41,750 --> 00:13:48,550
because i know this is a very popular question at interviews that i've actually encountered.

145
00:13:48,550 --> 00:13:51,269
so it's certainly an interesting problem to solve.

146
00:13:51,269 --> 00:13:55,910
so you mentioned being able to write effective code.

147
00:13:55,910 --> 00:14:01,910
and earlier in the episode you did talk about having learned python over a very short period of time.

148
00:14:01,910 --> 00:14:03,750
how do you then manage to write the effective code?

149
00:14:03,750 --> 00:14:07,829
is it like a back and forth between the code you write and you're learning?

150
00:14:07,839 --> 00:14:12,550
absolutely, it's a case of experiential learning or learning on the job.

151
00:14:12,560 --> 00:14:20,710
even if i had the time to engage in formal training in computer science it's probably more than i could ever possibly need for my purposes.

152
00:14:20,710 --> 00:14:26,710
so it's very much project-based learning for a social scientist in particular to become good at web scraping.

153
00:14:26,710 --> 00:14:30,629
so you definitely need a project that really really grabs you.

154
00:14:30,629 --> 00:14:37,590
and would sustain your intellectual interest long after you start encountering the challenges that i've mentioned with web scraping.

155
00:14:37,600 --> 00:14:44,069
it's definitely interesting to talk to you there because of the background and you know the fact.

156
00:14:44,069 --> 00:14:50,150
that the actual use case led you into learning the technologies for embarking on this journey.

157
00:14:50,160 --> 00:15:00,069
so in terms of reliability earlier you also mentioned the fact that some of these websites will have limits which you have to overcome.

158
00:15:00,069 --> 00:15:01,990
can you talk more about that?

159
00:15:01,990 --> 00:15:07,750
you know for that one specific case were you able to use that same methodology for every other case that you encountered?

160
00:15:07,750 --> 00:15:10,949
have you built that into the framework that you're using to do the web scraping?

161
00:15:10,959 --> 00:15:16,310
i'd like to say that all websites present the same challenges, but they do not.

162
00:15:16,310 --> 00:15:19,829
so in that particular use case the challenge was no matter who was making the request.

163
00:15:19,839 --> 00:15:29,110
after a certain amount of requests somewhere in the 1000 to 2000 requests in a row, the regulator's website would cancel any further requests.

164
00:15:29,110 --> 00:15:39,030
and wouldn't respond with a different regulator and a different jurisdiction it was a similar challenge but the solution was a little bit different this time.

165
00:15:39,030 --> 00:15:41,189
it was less to do with how many requests you made.

166
00:15:41,189 --> 00:15:47,269
and the fact that you couldn't make consecutive requests from the same ip address, so from the same computer or machine.

167
00:15:47,279 --> 00:15:54,629
so in that case i had to implement a solution which basically cycled through public proxies.

168
00:15:54,629 --> 00:16:00,069
so a public list of ip addresses and i would select from those and make my request using one of those ip addresses.

169
00:16:00,079 --> 00:16:05,590
cycle through the list again, make my request from a different ip address and so on and so forth.

170
00:16:05,590 --> 00:16:10,389
for the i think it was something like 10 or 15 000 requests i needed to make for records.

171
00:16:10,399 --> 00:16:17,990
so there are some common properties to some of the challenges, but actually the solutions need to be specific to the website.

172
00:16:17,990 --> 00:16:20,629
i see, what about the data quality?

173
00:16:20,629 --> 00:16:26,790
how do you know if you're not reading duplicate information which is in different pages or broken links?

174
00:16:26,800 --> 00:16:32,389
data quality thankfully is an area a lot of social scientists have a lot of experience with.

175
00:16:32,389 --> 00:16:37,590
so that particular aspect of web scraping is common.

176
00:16:37,590 --> 00:16:47,110
so whether i conduct a survey of of individuals, whether i collect data downloads run experiments and so on, the data quality challenges are largely the same.

177
00:16:47,110 --> 00:16:50,949
dealing with missing observations, dealing with duplicates, that's usually not problematic.

178
00:16:50,959 --> 00:16:57,829
what can be quite difficult is the updating of websites that does tend to happen reasonably frequently.

179
00:16:57,839 --> 00:17:03,430
if you're running your own little personal website then maybe it gets updated weekly or monthly.

180
00:17:03,440 --> 00:17:13,429
public service uk government website for example that gets updated multiple times across multiple web pages every day, sometimes on a minute basis.

181
00:17:13,439 --> 00:17:19,429
so for me you certainly have to build in some scheduling of your web scraping activities.

182
00:17:19,429 --> 00:17:25,590
but thankfully depending on the web page you're interested in there'll be some clues about how often the web page actually updates.

183
00:17:25,590 --> 00:17:32,950
so for regulators they have different policies about when they show the records of new non-profits.

184
00:17:32,950 --> 00:17:37,510
so some regulators say every day we get a new non-profit we'll update, some do it monthly.

185
00:17:37,510 --> 00:17:42,630
so usually there's persistent links and the information changes on a predictable basis.

186
00:17:42,630 --> 00:17:47,190
but of course there are definitely times where older web pages become obsolete.

187
00:17:47,200 --> 00:17:51,750
i'd like to say there's a sophisticated means i have of addressing that.

188
00:17:51,750 --> 00:17:59,350
but largely particularly for a non-programmer like myself it comes back to the detective work of frequently checking him at your scraper.

189
00:17:59,360 --> 00:18:07,190
making sure that the website is working as intended, looks as you expect, and making any necessary changes to your scraper.

190
00:18:07,200 --> 00:18:14,310
so in terms of maintenance of these tools have you done research in terms of how other people might be doing that?

191
00:18:14,310 --> 00:18:19,669
is there a lot of information available for you to rely on and learn?

192
00:18:19,679 --> 00:18:26,310
yes there are actually some free and some paid for solutions that do help you with the reliability of your scrapers.

193
00:18:26,320 --> 00:18:30,870
there's i think it is an australian product called morph dot io.

194
00:18:30,870 --> 00:18:37,270
which allows you to host your your scrapers, set a frequency with which the scrapers execute.

195
00:18:37,270 --> 00:18:44,070
and then there's a web page on the morph site which shows the results of your your scraper, how often it runs, what results it produces and so on.

196
00:18:44,070 --> 00:18:49,750
that does have some limitations, it means you have to make your results of your scraping and your scraper public.

197
00:18:49,750 --> 00:18:53,270
and you may not want to do that, particularly if you're a commercial institution.

198
00:18:53,280 --> 00:18:58,950
but there are other packages and software applications that do help you with the reliability.

199
00:18:58,960 --> 00:19:04,549
