00:37:21,599 --> 00:37:26,950
so it is certainly on those dynamic web pages as we've mentioned.

397
00:37:26,950 --> 00:37:29,430
it's certainly probably the major challenge to overcome.

398
00:37:29,430 --> 00:37:36,550
because as we've discussed there's ways around proxies and ways around making a limited number of requests and so on.

399
00:37:36,560 --> 00:37:41,510
captures are probably the outstanding problem certainly for academia and researchers.

400
00:37:41,520 --> 00:37:51,190
do you envision using machine learning natural language processing on the data that you're gathering sometime in the future if you haven't already?

401
00:37:51,200 --> 00:37:53,670
yes and no is the academics answer.

402
00:37:53,670 --> 00:37:58,790
in terms of machine learning for us that's the equivalent of statistical modeling.

403
00:37:58,790 --> 00:38:02,150
so that's you know trying to estimate the parameters that fit the data best.

404
00:38:02,160 --> 00:38:05,990
social scientists quantitative social scientists have similar tools.

405
00:38:05,990 --> 00:38:12,790
so different types of linear and logistic regression for example are very coherent with machine learning approaches.

406
00:38:12,800 --> 00:38:19,990
but certainly natural language processing is an enormously rich and valuable area for social science.

407
00:38:19,990 --> 00:38:24,870
as you said a lot of the information stored on web pages is unstructured and text.

408
00:38:24,880 --> 00:38:35,990
and making good sense of that and quantitatively analyzing the properties of the text and its meaning that is certainly the next big step i think for empirical social scientists.

409
00:38:36,000 --> 00:38:40,550
but i think machine learning we kind of have similar tools that we can implement.

410
00:38:40,560 --> 00:38:45,430
natural language is certainly something we don't currently do within our discipline.

411
00:38:45,430 --> 00:38:50,150
you know we don't have our own solutions and we certainly need that to help us make sense of of data that we screen.

412
00:38:50,160 --> 00:38:54,630
for the analytic aspects how much data do you feel that you need?

413
00:38:54,630 --> 00:39:02,630
and can you give an example of when you've used specifically use this and what kind of analysis have you gathered from the data you've captured?

414
00:39:02,640 --> 00:39:08,230
well one of the benefits of web scraping certainly for research purposes is it can be collected at a scale.

415
00:39:08,230 --> 00:39:16,950
that's very difficult to do through traditional means like surveys or focus groups interviews experiments and so on.

416
00:39:16,950 --> 00:39:22,790
so we can collect data in my case for entire non-profit sectors and then i can repeat that process for different jurisdictions.

417
00:39:22,800 --> 00:39:29,349
so when i've been looking at the impact of the pandemic on non-profit sectors for example.

418
00:39:29,349 --> 00:39:33,829
i'm collecting you know tens of thousands if not millions of records of for each jurisdiction.

419
00:39:33,829 --> 00:39:36,710
so thousands and tens of thousands of individual non-profits.

420
00:39:36,720 --> 00:39:45,109
then i'm aggregating all of that information you know into a time series of the number of charities or non-profits that are disappearing every month for example.

421
00:39:45,119 --> 00:39:48,550
i'm tracking that for a few years before the pandemic.

422
00:39:48,550 --> 00:39:51,030
so i have to have a good long time series in that direction.

423
00:39:51,040 --> 00:39:58,230
and i have to frequently collect data since the pandemic for these sectors as well.

424
00:39:58,230 --> 00:40:03,670
so then i'm tracking because of the pandemic are there now fewer charities being formed.

425
00:40:03,670 --> 00:40:08,069
and if there are does that mean that some needs will will go unmet because of that?

426
00:40:08,069 --> 00:40:10,470
so some communities may have a need for mental health services.

427
00:40:10,480 --> 00:40:17,430
and if there are now fewer mental health charities being formed what's the impact and what kind of planning you know should government government do?

428
00:40:17,440 --> 00:40:22,150
and then the flip side if more charities are now disappearing as a result of the pandemic.

429
00:40:22,160 --> 00:40:29,750
then what impact is that going to have on public services in certain communities also?

430
00:40:29,750 --> 00:40:35,510
so to be able to answer what seems to be reasonably simple you know understandable questions does need large-scale data.

431
00:40:35,520 --> 00:40:42,710
that's processed collected frequently and then collapsed into you know aggregate measures over time.

432
00:40:42,720 --> 00:40:50,390
that can be done in python that can be done in any particular programming or statistical software package.

433
00:40:50,390 --> 00:40:56,390
my personal preference is to use python for data collection, i think it has lots of computational advantages to doing that.

434
00:40:56,400 --> 00:41:02,470
and i kind of like to use traditional social science packages for the analysis also.

435
00:41:02,470 --> 00:41:04,470
but again that's entirely a personal preference.

436
00:41:04,470 --> 00:41:09,030
and everything can be done in open source software, the whole data collection cleaning and analysis.

437
00:41:09,040 --> 00:41:13,510
would be curious to hear what packages did you use for this?

438
00:41:13,520 --> 00:41:22,309
well i used the stata statistical software package which is a proprietary piece of software by a company in texas.

439
00:41:22,309 --> 00:41:27,750
and that has been built for the types of analysis that quantitative social scientists tend to do.

440
00:41:27,750 --> 00:41:34,390
so regressions, time series analyses, survival analyses these kind of things that we traditionally do.

441
00:41:34,400 --> 00:41:40,390
those are now being imported into the likes of python and or so it.

442
00:41:40,390 --> 00:41:42,550
as i said it is getting possible to do everything in a single language.

443
00:41:42,560 --> 00:41:49,270
but certainly i can't do any of the web scraping within the traditional tools that i've been using stata or spss for example.

444
00:41:49,280 --> 00:41:52,390
so i guess i'm building a workflow of different tools.

445
00:41:52,400 --> 00:41:58,390
tools that i think are particularly good for each distinct task rather than trying to do everything in a single tool.

446
00:41:58,400 --> 00:42:06,150
make sense, could you still talk more about what happens once you start using the tool that you've done?

447
00:42:06,150 --> 00:42:11,109
what kind of aggregations then do you try to use the tool for?

448
00:42:11,109 --> 00:42:12,950
what kind of input additional input you might have to provide?

449
00:42:12,950 --> 00:42:18,069
would be interested to kind of close that loop here.

450
00:42:18,069 --> 00:42:22,069
i see, of course web scraping is simply stage one of completing this piece of analysis.

451
00:42:22,069 --> 00:42:28,230
so once i transfer the raw data into stata which is what i use then it begins a data cleaning process.

452
00:42:28,240 --> 00:42:33,750
which is centered really around collapsing the data into aggregate measures.

453
00:42:33,750 --> 00:42:37,910
so the raw data every row is a non-profit and there's a date field.

454
00:42:37,910 --> 00:42:41,030
so a date of registration or a date of of dissolution.

455
00:42:41,040 --> 00:42:50,470
so i'm collapsing all of those individual records into monthly observations of the number of non-profits who are formed and are dissolved in a given month.

456
00:42:50,480 --> 00:42:56,470
analytically then the approach i'm using is that data forms a time series.

457
00:42:56,470 --> 00:42:59,430
so there's x number of charities formed in a given month.

458
00:42:59,440 --> 00:43:03,589
then we have what we would call an exogenous shock which is the pandemic.

459
00:43:03,589 --> 00:43:08,150
so this is you know something that was not predictable at least analytically.

460
00:43:08,150 --> 00:43:12,230
we may have arguments about whether it was predictable from a policy perspective.

461
00:43:12,230 --> 00:43:19,190
so we essentially have an experiment where we have a before period which is you know almost like the control group.

462
00:43:19,190 --> 00:43:21,349
and we have the pandemic period which is like the treatment group.

463
00:43:21,359 --> 00:43:31,510
and then we're seeing if that time series of the number of non-profits that are formed is discontinued or disrupted because of the pandemic.

464
00:43:31,510 --> 00:43:38,790
so we have a technique called interrupted time series analysis which is a quasi-experimental research design and mode of analysis.

465
00:43:38,790 --> 00:43:44,150
and then that gives us an estimate of to what degree the number of charities has now changed.

466
00:43:44,160 --> 00:43:49,589
and whether the long-term temporal trend has changed also.

467
00:43:49,589 --> 00:43:58,710
so to give a specific example from what we've just concluded is that the pandemic certainly led to many fewer charities being dissolved.

468
00:43:58,720 --> 00:44:06,230
so that sounds a bit counterintuitive, you would think such a big economic shock would lead to more nonprofit organizations actually disappearing.

469
00:44:06,230 --> 00:44:12,710
the opposite happened, we actually had much fewer dissolutions than we would expect from the pre-pandemic trend.

470
00:44:12,720 --> 00:44:19,670
so there's been a massive shock in the level, a massive change in the level, but the long-term trend is the same.

471
00:44:19,670 --> 00:44:25,109
so over time there's not been much deviation in the number of charities dissolving and we see that going forward as well.

472
00:44:25,109 --> 00:44:31,030
so it's like a one-off shock, it's like a one-off drop in the number but the long-term trend continues.

473
00:44:31,030 --> 00:44:39,109
and specifically that if you're interested the reason is the pandemic affected regulators who processed the applications of charities to dissolve.

474
00:44:39,119 --> 00:44:47,030
a lot of their activities were halted so they couldn't process the applications and hence we have lower levels.

475
00:44:47,030 --> 00:44:52,710
and that's in combination with the fact that a lot of governments around the world put in place financial support packages.

476
00:44:52,710 --> 00:45:01,750
that kept organizations that would naturally fail if that makes sense, it prevented them from doing so and kept them afloat for a much longer period than we would expect.

477
00:45:01,760 --> 00:45:07,990
so at some point we're expecting a reversion to the the level but it hasn't happened yet.

